## Controlled comparison and what it establishes

The disposable Sargo comparison used the same sealed kernel/userspace inputs and varied the allocator-selection parameter. Its native workload was initialization, 1,000 8-KiB allocation/free operations, deep sleep, TEE close and loader shutdown. One attempt with no fastboot device and one user-confirmed power-off are excluded from failure evidence.

The uninterrupted original-allocator run completed the native sequence and exited 0. Loader stop began at 45.828 seconds; RPMh timeout appeared at 56.095 seconds; a spontaneous reset followed at approximately 58.97 seconds. No coordinator reset was requested. Local run: out/liveboot/runs/sargo-fingerprint-pool-baseline-04-20260913; UART SHA256 7ab618b992579dfe7c2be22b662ea9d3a7c1a2c6aee990cd94723f591ded2889.

The pool-reuse comparison completed the same sequence and all three loader stops, staying responsive until an explicit diagnostic reboot at 366.032 seconds. Local run: out/liveboot/runs/sargo-fingerprint-pool-reuse-04-20260913; UART SHA256 1e9f939bc19062a97cd388164edb80d237864f7667127dfb3ebd532262f95279.

Repetition passed 50 native cycles across ten firmware lifetimes, 50,000 allocation/free operations and 30 service stops in 341.0719 seconds. UART HELP responded at 447.225 seconds; the operator explicitly rebooted at 530.331 seconds. No observed stall signatures or spontaneous reset. Local run: out/liveboot/runs/sargo-fingerprint-pool-repeat-05-20260913; UART SHA256 fdf4b608f1e09d24010038f3e416816dc20aa9eaa7b250d1d0fd7462e1e4fc21.

This supports the effectiveness of retaining coherent backing mappings in the device-owned TZ pool. It does not prove the exact platform fault. Each command still clears and releases its individual staging allocation; userspace-mapped TEE memory remains separate. No fingerprint modification to qcom_tzmem.c was required.

## Installed and physical acceptance, September 13

The signed .12 packages were incorporated into the local accepted image after package/boot/PAM/policy comparisons. The image preserved the existing PIN route, encrypted homed and unrelated phone packages; SELinux was enforcing.

The installed controller performed ordinary fprintd Claim/Release only, using packaged libfprint. Fifty cycles all returned 0. Ten complete lifetimes each stopped fprintd, Keymaster startup, FPC app loader, cmnlib64 loader and qsee-supplicant: 50 explicit stops in 58.3538077 seconds. UART independently recorded completions. It started no capture and performed no credential operation. The temporary timeout override was removed.

The user then enrolled additional fingers, reopened Settings roughly a dozen times and checked correct/incorrect-finger feedback. One authorized normal reboot retained enrollment metadata and fingerprint socket activation. After initial PIN login, the user confirmed approximately six mixed cycles of multiple-finger Phosh unlock, wrong-finger rejection, PIN fallback and reopening Settings' tester. The phone remained responsive on the same boot; no new UART stall signatures appeared. Historical success is not a claim about today's deployment or indefinite soak reliability.

